  "strategy": "Based on the analysis, a new approach is proposed that utilizes sophisticated signal processing and optimization techniques to further lower the upper bound. Directly optimizing the step function is challenging due to the non-convex nature of the problem. However, the problem can be reframed using the Fourier transform, which directly relates the autocorrelation (convolution) of a signal to its spectral properties. Specifically, the objective function measures the maximum value of the autocorrelation of the normalized step function.\n\nThe key idea is to optimize the spectral profile of the step function in the frequency domain, which can be decomposed into components that transform readily. This is inspired by the principle that controlling the frequency spectrum can effectively manage the autocorrelation properties. We use periodic sequences of specific lengths and phases to target multiple Fourier modes, ensuring the resulting step function satisfies all constraints.\n\nThe new algorithm involves the following major steps:\n1. Initialization via Fourier Transform: Create sequences by shaping their frequency domain representation (sinc^4 or Chebyshev).\n2.Adaptive Periodicity Scaling: Explore sequences of varying lengths using interpolation but preserving the fundamental Fourier structures.\n3.Intensified Fourier-domain Optimization: Apply a highly tuned L-BFGS-B optimization to sequences derived from phase-space manipulations.\n4.Multiple-frequency Lean Optimization: Use gradient descent to minimize the autocorrelation across frequencies.\n\nThe evaluation function will be called frequently to check for improvements, ensuring only valid sequences are considered.",
